;Match details

* '''Match 1: Tanaka def. Reyes.''' The Bulldog's rebound. Tanaka, 0-1 after the tournament-opening loss to KAZUKI, needed a result and fought like it — dragging Reyes into a brawl on the floor within the first two minutes. Reyes's joint work nearly turned it: the Cobra Twist had Tanaka's shoulders trapped at 9 minutes, and the Bulldog barely crawled to the ropes. The finish: Reyes went for the Cobra Twist a second time — Tanaka backed him into the corner to break the grip, then hit the Bulldog Bomb at 11:12. Tanaka is 1-1; Reyes is 0-2 — the new tag champion is still searching for his first singles win in the tournament. '''Crowd:''' HEAT for Tanaka. POP for Reyes's entrance. TENSION during the Cobra Twist. GASPS for the Bulldog Bomb. BOOS for the corner break — the crowd saw the shortcut. Mild applause for Reyes as he left.

* '''Match 2: Ishida def. Diallo.''' The veteran's answer. Eight days after Jusuf submitted him in 8:56 — the fastest loss of the tournament — Ishida walked to the ring to a reception he did not expect: the Tokuyama crowd stood. The match was the plan executed: Ishida refused to trade power with the Hammer, circled him, chopped him down with leg kicks, and targeted the left arm from the first minute. Diallo's power still landed — a Hammer Slam attempt at 10 minutes that Ishida escaped by slipping behind the giant's back and hitting a dragon screw. The finish: Diallo went for the Hammer Slam at 13 minutes; Ishida shifted his weight mid-lift, slid down the giant's back, and locked in the Ishida Lock in the center of the ring. Diallo held on for 25 seconds, then tapped at 13:08. Ishida's first points. The crowd's ovation was the loudest of the night until the main event. '''Crowd:''' STANDING OVATION for Ishida's entrance — the crowd remembered Day 2. '''ISHIDA''' chants throughout. GASPS for the dragon screw counter. ERUPTION when the Ishida Lock locked in. '''TAP''' chants — then applause when Diallo tapped. '''ISHIDA''' chants as he left. Ishida paused at the curtain and nodded to the crowd.

* '''Match 3: Taumafa def. Kuroki.''' Strong style vs. family power. Kuroki — 0-1 after the Day 2 classic with Takagi — came to brawl, and for ten minutes it worked: his forearms staggered the giant for the first time in the tournament, and a lariat at 11 minutes put Taumafa down for 2.7 — the biggest near-fall against him so far. But Taumafa's power broke through: the samoan drop, the running hip attack, and at 15 minutes the giant powerbomb. Clean pin at 15:34. Taumafa is 2-0 and quietly perfect; Kuroki is 0-2 with two strong performances and nothing to show for them. '''Crowd:''' POP for Taumafa. '''KUROKI''' chants during the striking exchanges. GASPS when the lariat put the giant down. ERUPTION for the giant powerbomb. Respectful applause for Kuroki — the crowd knows his record lies.

* '''Match 4: KAZUKI def. Ono.''' The semi-main — the young lion's speed against the Shooting Star's precision. Ono, 0-1, fought with nothing to lose: dropkicks to the knee, a tornado DDT, and at 12 minutes the flash cradle — the same small package that nearly beat Ishida at [[NOPW Wednesday Episode 4|Episode 4]] — for 2.8. KAZUKI kicked out, reset, and took over: the Pele kick, the missile dropkick, the Twin Star Press at 14:22. Clean pin. After the match, KAZUKI helped Ono up and spoke briefly to him — the crowd could not hear the words, but Ono nodded. KAZUKI is 2-0; Ono is 0-2 with the crowd fully behind him. '''Crowd:''' MEGA POP for KAZUKI. '''ONO''' chants by the midpoint. ERUPTION at the 2.8 flash cradle — half the building thought it was over. BIG POP for the Twin Star Press. Warm applause for the post-match exchange.

* '''Match 5: Takagi def. Jusuf.''' The main event — and the story of Block B's first week. Jusuf, 1-0 after the 8:56 destruction of Ishida, had never been pinned and never been made to bump. Takagi's plan was simple and violent: make the strikes too real to no-sell. The opening minute set the terms — Takagi's forearm across the jaw, and Jusuf went down. The first bump of Jusuf's NOPW career. The crowd gasped, then roared. Jusuf rose slowly, stared, and answered with the mosaraza — the kneebar locked in at 6 minutes, and for the first time Takagi was in real trouble, dragging himself to the ropes at 7:30. The match settled into its rhythm: Jusuf's grappling against Takagi's strikes, each man's truth. At 14 minutes Jusuf locked the kneebar a second time — Takagi reached the ropes again, and this time pulled himself up and answered with a forearm so stiff the crowd went quiet. The finish: the Dragon suplex at 15:50 — Jusuf down again — then the Dragon lariat at 16:41. Clean pin. Jusuf's first loss. The Tokuyama crowd gave both men a standing ovation. Jusuf sat up, stared at Takagi, and walked out without acknowledgment. Takagi is 2-0 and the Dragon is the block's spoiler; Jusuf is 1-1 and the questions have begun. '''Crowd:''' HEAT for Jusuf's entrance. BUILD for Takagi. ERUPTION at the first bump — the loudest pop of the night. TENSION during both kneebars. Silence, then roars for the final forearm. ERUPTION for the Dragon lariat. Standing ovation. '''TAKAGI''' chants as Jusuf walked out.

